y and the defense 23rd in EPA allowed, so neither side has stood out much yet.","offense":"The Raiders offense has been about average by EPA per play so far, and it has been much better passing the ball than running it. Their run and pass mix has stayed close to what game situations call for. Kirk Cousins has run the passing game, with Brock Bowers leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Raiders have one of the worst defenses in the league this season, ranking 25th or worse in three of the five main efficiency measures. Their one bright spot has been takeaways per game, where they rank 2nd. Maxx Crosby has been as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise is Kwity Paye, who has 3 sacks, after not being a regular on this defense last season. Their scheme has been fairly conventional, and the closest thing to a signature is a number of defenders in the box that ranks 8th lowest in the league.","special":"The Raiders have been about average in how often they go for it on fourth down. Their kickers have made 89% of field goals, which is a little above average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the defensive line, about 28% of the money on the books.","players":"Kirk Cousins has played nearly every snap at quarterback and thrown for 1,877 yards. Targets have been spread around, with Brock Bowers and Tre Tucker leading a group where nobody has more than a fifth of the looks. Ashton Jeanty has handled most of the work in the backfield with 67% of the carries. On defense, Kwity Paye leads the team with 3 sacks and Hezekiah Masses has picked off 3 passes.","coaching":"Klint Kubiak is calling the offense as head coach and Rob Leonard is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2023 Seahawks and the 2022 Broncos. Their defense looks most like the 2023 Saints under Dennis Allen and the 2023 Dolphins under Vic Fangio.","trends":"The offense went from 32nd in EPA per play in 2025 to 17th so far this season, a big step forward. The defense moved from 21st in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 23rd so far this season, about where it was. The biggest change in approach was number of backs in the backfield, which went from 1.07 to 1.23.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}